big headline from the conservative  manifesto 50,000 more nurses on our  overstretched hospital wards bursaries  for nurse training will be brought back  but the funding for it has already been  questioned and one of the things that we  can do with this new package is not just  have the bursaries for nurses and people  who want to become nurses but also have  the place is the training places in  universities and hospitals and the  apprenticeships that we want to use to  recruit so it's more than just the  bursary it's also about having those  training places available opposition  parties claim the numbers don't add up  Labor's shadow health secretary Jonathan  Ashworth says the pledge is frankly  deceitful and the Lib Dems Luciana  Berger called the figures insulting to  the public so what's the argument about  of the fifty thousand promised nurses  fourteen thousand will be new graduates  and undergraduates coming into the  system grants for trainee nurses which  was scrapped in 2017 by the  Conservatives will be partially  reinstated a u-turn by the government  after the move led to a drop in nursing  applications but their tuition fees are  not all covered a further 12 and a half  thousand nurses will be recruited from  abroad through a new NHS visa and it's  hoped that around a third of the total  eighteen and a half thousand will be  existing nurses either staying in the  profession or returning to it because of  improved career opportunities a few  years ago the current government removed  the nursing bursary which meant we saw a  really significant decrease in the  number of applicants coming into nursing  what they pledged that they're making  for should they get into government's  next time it goes some way to replacing  half of what they took away and those  criticisms were quickly jumped on by the  opposition who say new nurses will have  to be found elsewhere  well since they're a party that's  presided over 40,000 nurse vacancies in  the NHS who three years ago said there  was going to put more doctors in the NHS  and there are now six thousand five  hundred less doctors or vacancies of  doctors in the NHS they haven't put the  money into nurse bursaries they haven't  put the money into education and  training they are simply proved they're  unable to do it  Forrest Johnson was all smiles as he  launched his manifesto claiming  delivering brexit would allow the  government to focus on public services  like the NHS but can big figure promises  translate into action  [Applause]
 

